## Reader's guide

**Neuroevolution through Elixir**  
Part II - Neuroevolution: Taking the First Step

**One genotype. Three executable phenotypes.** First preserve Sher's process
architecture, then express the same computation in Nx, then put the tensor network
inside a supervised actor. Compare the results before comparing the costs.

This notebook remasters Gene I. Sher's Chapter 6, printed pp. 153-185, from the
supplied PDF. Its first PDF page is the introduction to Part II; PDF page 2 is
printed p. 153. References marked **[S]** refer to that source. Sections **6.1-6.7**
retain its progression. The tensor and hybrid implementations, safety checks,
comparison experiments, and GES commentary are **remaster additions**.

The source builds a **static**, fully connected, layered network with one sensor
and one actuator. We keep that scope. There is no training, topology mutation,
fitness selection, or claim of intelligence in this chapter. Those come later.

### The three passes

| Pass               | What represents the phenotype?                            | What stays the same?                                   |
| ------------------ | --------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------ |
| Thesis: actor      | A sensor, a process per neuron, an actuator, and a cortex | Ordered inputs, weights, biases, tanh, outputs         |
| Antithesis: tensor | A tuple of weight/bias tensors and an Nx numerical graph  | The same genotype and numerical mapping                |
| Synthesis: hybrid  | One supervised network actor containing that tensor graph | The same tensor kernel; a different lifecycle boundary |

The complete actor implementation comes first in 6.6A, followed by the complete
Nx implementation in 6.6B and the hybrid in 6.6C. Earlier sections establish the
neuron, interfaces, architecture, genotype, and constructor used by all three.

### Run this notebook

Evaluate the setup cell, then the remaining cells in order. No previous notebook
is needed. Elixir 1.17 or later in the 1.x series is required by the pinned Nx
release. Initial package installation needs network access or a prepared cache.
The experiments themselves use no external data. [D1]

Every **computed data table** has Interactive, Markdown, and CSV tabs. Explanatory
tables remain ordinary Markdown. Every handwritten named function has an `@spec`;
anonymous callbacks are covered by their enclosing API's types.

The reference configuration uses `Nx.BinaryBackend` and `Nx.Defn.Evaluator` with
`f64` tensors. The hybrid worker sets these defaults in its own process too.
The benchmark measures this configuration, not GPU acceleration. Changing
backends requires updating both the setup and `Chapter06.Hybrid.init/1`.

All network examples close their owned processes in `after` blocks. They use no
globally registered neuron or cortex names. A deliberate worker-failure test may
produce an OTP diagnostic. It is separate from normal inference.

**Validation status:** independent Python/NumPy reference checks covered the source
neuron result, matrix packing, scalar/dense parity, shuffled signal delivery,
and parameter round-trips. Static notebook checks were also performed. These
reference models are not an execution of the Elixir code. Elixir/BEAM and
Livebook are unavailable there: the Elixir cells, OTP behavior, and rendered Kino
widgets have **not** been executed here. The notebook's own checks and benchmark
produce actual results in your runtime; no timing results are invented.

