### E. Measure without preselecting a winner

There are two comparisons: **one request per observation**, and **one request per
batch**. Compare actor, tensor, and hybrid in the first group; compare tensor and
hybrid in the second. Do not attribute the entire difference between a batched
kernel and a scalar actor to message passing alone.

Inputs and tensor conversion are prepared outside the steady-state timing.
Every invocation shape is warmed and numerically checked. Tensor results are
materialized with `Nx.to_flat_list/1` inside the timed function, so the measured
work includes obtaining completed numerical output. These are local exploratory
measurements, not a statistical performance study.

The separate setup table measures process creation/wiring or tensor packing;
it does not claim to measure a clean, cold compiler cache. "Owned processes"
counts application-owned workers and their supervisor, not all BEAM or library
processes. The numerical-only version therefore reports zero in that column.

```elixir
defmodule Chapter06.Benchmark do
  alias Chapter06.{Actor, Genotype, Hybrid, Support, Tensor}
  @moduledoc "Measures completed work; results are produced locally, not predicted."

  @spec run(Genotype.t(), [[number()]], pos_integer()) :: {[map()], [map()]}
  def run(genotype, tape, repetitions) do
    unless tape != [] and repetitions > 0, do: raise(ArgumentError, "nonempty workload required")
    x = Nx.tensor(tape, type: :f64)
    x_rows = Enum.map(tape, &Nx.tensor([&1], type: :f64))
    {actor_setup, actor} = :timer.tc(fn -> Actor.open(genotype) end)
    try do
      {tensor_setup, params} = :timer.tc(fn -> Tensor.compile(genotype) end)
      {hybrid_setup, hybrid} = :timer.tc(fn -> Hybrid.open(genotype) end)
      try do
        workloads = [
          {"Actor", "single", fn -> Actor.run(actor, tape) |> List.flatten() end},
          {"Tensor", "single", fn -> Enum.flat_map(x_rows, &(Tensor.forward(params, &1) |> Nx.to_flat_list())) end},
          {"Hybrid", "single", fn -> Enum.flat_map(x_rows, &(Hybrid.forward(hybrid, &1) |> Nx.to_flat_list())) end},
          {"Tensor", "batch", fn -> Tensor.forward(params, x) |> Nx.to_flat_list() end},
          {"Hybrid", "batch", fn -> Hybrid.forward(hybrid, x) |> Nx.to_flat_list() end}
        ]
        expected = Tensor.forward(params, x) |> Nx.to_flat_list() |> Nx.tensor(type: :f64)
        # Warm every invocation shape and check parity before measuring it.
        Enum.each(workloads, fn {_name, _mode, fun} ->
          unless Support.close?(Nx.tensor(fun.(), type: :f64), expected, 1.0e-10) do
            raise "benchmark parity check failed"
          end
        end)
        timings = Enum.map(workloads, fn {name, mode, fun} ->
          samples = Enum.map(1..repetitions, fn _ -> elem(:timer.tc(fun), 0) end) |> Enum.sort()
          median = Enum.at(samples, div(length(samples), 2))
          %{implementation: name, mode: mode, examples: length(tape), repetitions: repetitions,
            median_us: median, us_per_example: median / length(tape)}
        end)
        setup = [
          %{implementation: "Actor", setup_us: actor_setup, owned_processes: map_size(actor.by_id) + 1},
          %{implementation: "Tensor", setup_us: tensor_setup, owned_processes: 0},
          %{implementation: "Hybrid", setup_us: hybrid_setup, owned_processes: 2}
        ]
        {timings, setup}
      after
        Hybrid.close(hybrid)
      end
    after
      Actor.close(actor)
    end
  end
end
```

