  Map.put(row, :label, row.implementation <> " / " <> row.mode)
end)
fig_benchmark = Vl.new(width: 640, height: 280,
  title: "R6.4 - Local steady-state time per example (BinaryBackend)")
  |> Vl.data_from_values(tbl_benchmark_plot)
  |> Vl.mark(:bar)
  |> Vl.encode_field(:x, "label", type: :nominal, title: "Implementation / invocation mode")
  |> Vl.encode_field(:y, "us_per_example", type: :quantitative,
    title: "Microseconds per example; lower is faster")
  |> Vl.encode_field(:color, "mode", type: :nominal)
fig_benchmark
```

Small networks on BinaryBackend may favor scalar arithmetic. A larger batch or
a native numerical backend may change the result. This notebook makes neither
outcome a requirement. Repeat measurements, record the backend and machine,
separate initialization from steady state, and compare equal workloads before
making an architectural claim.

## 6.7 Summary

*Source: [S], pp. 184-185. The comparative synthesis and checks are additions.*

Sher's progression is now complete: neuron -> sensor/neuron/actuator system ->
architecture -> genotype -> constructor -> mapper -> live execution -> backup.
We have retained that progression and implemented three execution choices.

| Question                              | Actor                                     | Tensor                                      | Hybrid                             |
| ------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------- |
| Where are weights owned?              | Individual neuron processes               | Explicit parameter tuple                    | A network worker's state           |
| How is computation scheduled?         | Messages and a cortex barrier             | Tensor graph dependencies                   | A request invokes the tensor graph |
| What can be checkpointed?             | ID-mapped runtime parameters              | Tensor parameters converted back to records | The worker's tensor parameters     |
| What does a worker failure mean here? | Abort and rebuild the whole wired network | No neural worker exists to restart          | Recreate the network worker        |
| What remains to be added?             | Optimization and evolution                | Optimization and evolution                  | Optimization and evolution         |

**Thesis-antithesis-synthesis is our implementation method. GES is our
evolutionary method.** They answer different questions.

| GES stage  | What Chapter 6 supplies                              | What it does not yet supply                   |
| ---------- | ---------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------- |
| Generation | A constructor for legal candidate networks           | Evolutionary variation or offspring policies  |
| Evaluation | Executable phenotypes and inspectable action vectors | A task-specific fitness or novelty measure    |
| Selection  | Nothing yet                                          | Survival, reproduction, and archive decisions |

### Executable checks

Run this cell after the preceding experiments. Checks fail loudly rather than
printing a plausible-looking table after a mismatch. They cover the demonstrated
scope, not arbitrary neural graphs or all backend/runtime combinations.

```elixir
# Deliberately changing a parameter is a backup test, not a training algorithm.
{first_w, first_b} = elem(mod_tensor, 0)
mod_modified = put_elem(mod_tensor, 0, {first_w, Nx.add(first_b, 0.125)})
genotype_modified = Chapter06.Tensor.backup(genotype_demo, mod_modified)
y_modified = Chapter06.Tensor.forward(mod_modified, x_demo)
y_recompiled = Chapter06.Tensor.forward(Chapter06.Tensor.compile(genotype_modified), x_demo)

{genotype_repeat, _} = Chapter06.Constructor.construct(:rng, :pts, [1, 3], constructor_rng)
parameter_count = Enum.reduce(tbl_shapes, 0, &(&1.parameters + &2))

genotype_bad = Enum.map(genotype_demo, fn
  %{kind: :neuron} = n ->
    [{id, weights} | rest] = n.input_idps
    %{n | input_idps: [{id, weights ++ [0.0]} | rest]}
  other -> other
end)

boundary_checks = Chapter06.Actor.using(genotype_demo, fn actor ->
  empty = Chapter06.Actor.run(actor, [])
  before = Chapter06.Actor.status(actor)
  rejected = Chapter06.Support.raises_argument?(fn -> Chapter06.Actor.step(actor, [1.0]) end)
  {:ok, output} = Chapter06.Actor.step(actor, hd(tape_demo))
  after_step = Chapter06.Actor.status(actor)
  %{zero: empty == [] and before.completed == 0,
    bad_input_rejected: rejected, still_healthy: after_step.completed == 1 and after_step.halted == nil,
    output: output}
end)

coexist = Chapter06.Actor.using(genotype_single, fn a ->
  Chapter06.Actor.using(genotype_single, fn b ->
    {:ok, ya} = Chapter06.Actor.step(a, [1.0, 2.0])
    {:ok, yb} = Chapter06.Actor.step(b, [1.0, 2.0])
    a.cortex != b.cortex and ya == yb
  end)
end)

csv_probe = Chapter06.Tables.csv([%{a: "comma, quote\" and line\n"}], [:a])
checks = [
  {abs(result_single.output - 0.25441202264242263) < 1.0e-12, "Printed single-neuron result"},
  {abs(hd(actor_single) - result_single.output) < 1.0e-12, "Four-role network agrees with simple neuron"},
  {abs(Nx.to_number(y_single_tensor) - result_single.output) < 1.0e-12, "Nx neuron agrees with the printed example"},
  {length(Chapter06.Genotype.one(genotype_demo, :cortex).nids) == 5, "Hidden [1,3] creates five neurons"},
  {parameter_count == 13, "Constructor creates thirteen parameters including biases"},
  {genotype_repeat == genotype_demo, "Explicit RNG state reproduces the genotype"},
  {genotype_reloaded == genotype_demo, "Checkpoint encode/decode preserves the genotype"},
  {Chapter06.Support.raises_argument?(fn -> Chapter06.Genotype.validate!(genotype_bad) end), "Bad weight dimensions are rejected"},
  {Chapter06.Support.raises_argument?(fn -> Chapter06.Constructor.new(2, [0], 1, constructor_rng) end), "Zero-width hidden layer is rejected"},
  {actor_demo.status.completed == length(tape_demo), "Exactly N requested actor cycles complete"},
  {actor_demo.status.busy == false and actor_demo.status.halted == nil, "Actor finishes at a healthy barrier"},
  {actor_demo.backup == genotype_demo, "Actor backup maps runtime PIDs back to IDs"},
  {Nx.shape(y_tensor) == {length(tape_demo), 1}, "Tensor output preserves batch and actuator dimensions"},
  {Chapter06.Support.close?(y_actor, y_tensor, 1.0e-10), "Actor and tensor numerical parity"},
  {Chapter06.Support.close?(y_hybrid, y_tensor, 1.0e-10), "Hybrid and tensor numerical parity"},
  {hybrid_demo.status.calls == 1 and hybrid_demo.status.examples == length(tape_demo), "One hybrid batch call accounts for all examples"},
  {hybrid_demo.backup == genotype_demo, "Hybrid parameter backup round-trip"},
  {Chapter06.Tensor.backup(genotype_demo, mod_tensor) == genotype_demo, "Tensor parameter backup round-trip"},
  {genotype_modified != genotype_demo, "Modified tensor parameters reach the backup"},
  {Chapter06.Support.close?(y_modified, y_recompiled, 1.0e-10), "Modified backup replays the modified network"},
  {Enum.all?(tbl_shape_parity, &(&1.actor_error < 1.0e-10 and &1.tensor_error < 1.0e-10 and &1.hybrid_error < 1.0e-10)), "All tested shapes match the scalar reference"},
  {Enum.all?(tbl_shape_parity, &(&1.cycles == &1.examples)), "Cycle count remains correct across shapes"},
  {Enum.all?(tbl_shape_parity, & &1.backups_equal), "All tested shapes preserve their parameters"},
  {Enum.all?(tbl_shape_parity, & &1.cleaned_up), "Both actor architectures release owned processes"},
  {abs(ordering_result.neuron - ordering_result.expected) < 1.0e-12, "Reverse arrival and duplicate sender do not change the neuron"},
  {ordering_result.ordered_actuator == [7.0, 22.0], "Actuator order and epoch isolation"},
  {failure_result.rejected, "Worker failure produces an operational error"},
  {failure_result.cleaned_up, "Failed network's remaining processes are closed"},
  {failure_result.restored_error < 1.0e-10, "Fresh mapping reproduces the stored network"},
  {boundary_checks.zero, "Zero requested cycles produce zero actions"},
  {boundary_checks.bad_input_rejected and boundary_checks.still_healthy, "Invalid input is rejected before disturbing the running network"},
  {coexist, "Independent networks coexist without global names"},
  {is_binary(csv_probe) and String.ends_with?(csv_probe, "\r\n"), "CSV callback content is a CRLF-terminated binary"}
]
tbl_checks = Enum.map(checks, fn {passed, label} -> Chapter06.Support.check!(passed, label) end)
Chapter06.Tables.tabs(tbl_checks, [:check, :result], "ch_06_tbl_12_executable_checks.csv")
```

### Exercises

| Change                        | Prediction to write before running                          | Check                                                        |
| ----------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |
| Use `hidden = []`             | Which layer now receives the sensor vector?                 | Output shape, parameter count, parity                        |
| Give the actuator two outputs | Which neuron determines each output position?               | Reverse-arrival ordering test and shape suite                |
| Change one bias, then back up | Which record must change?                                   | Recompile and compare outputs                                |
| Increase input-batch size     | Which request overheads are amortized?                      | Compare single-request and batch groups separately           |
| Kill a neuron                 | Does restarting only that PID repair its neighbors' routes? | Explain why this implementation rebuilds the whole phenotype |
| Add a recurrent edge on paper | Which cycle semantics would need to change?                 | Do not bypass the feedforward validator                      |

**The main insight:** an inherited neural design and its runtime realization are
separable. Actors, tensors, and a hybrid can implement the same signal-processing
contract while exposing different ownership, scheduling, and failure boundaries.

**Next:** Chapter 7 adds stochastic hill climbing. The three phenotype paths can
then share a perturbation/evaluation protocol instead of independently changing
the optimization problem.

